  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 115 AM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A few frontal systems will bring isolated to \nscattered storms Thursday through Sat.\n\nThe anomalous upper ridge over the TN valley is forecast to \ngradually shift west toward the lower MS valley late this week \nand weekend. As that happens, northwest flow around the \nperiphery of the ridge may direct a few convectively-induced \ndisturbances into the region today through early Sat ahead of a \nfew frontal systems. Moisture levels will correspondingly \nincrease to well above seasonal normals with precipitable water \nvalues around 1.7 to 2 inches during this period.\n\nEnsemble and deterministic guidance shows isolated showers or \nstorms developing over the lower OH valley into the Appalachians\nof NC/VA this afternoon and evening in association with a \nconvectively- induced disturbance in the developing NW flow \naloft. Instability today will be confined west of central NC in \nVA and far western NC. Any showers that do develop, albeit \nminimal, are only expected to briefly impact the Triad and along\nthe VA border of the northern/northwest Piedmont.\n\nA better chance of showers and storms will exist Friday into \nSaturday as a more prominent lead trough and backdoor cold front\nmove south from the northeast late Friday through early \nSaturday. Instability increases to 1000-1500 J/kg, though lapse \nrates and shear will limit storm intensity. Storms will maximize\nduring the early to mid evening Friday over central NC. On \nSaturday, as the front moves through, the best chance of storms \nwill focus over the southern Piedmont, Sandhills, and southern \nCoastal Plain along residual instability and any lingering \nconvergence if the front is slow to move through. Either way, NE\nflow should favor lower temperatures in the mid 80s Saturday, \ncontrasted with lower 90s Friday. We cannot rule out some stray \nisolated storms Sunday, but that part of the forecast appears to\nbe trending drier.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... An unsettled pattern with more seasonable \ntemperatures may return early to middle of next week, though details \nare unclear at this point.\n\nFrom Sunday onward, ensemble guidance continues to show a \npotentially unsettled pattern, albeit with low predictability. \nRegardless of which ensemble cluster solution you examine, they \nall appear to agree that northeast flow will re-exert itself \nTuesday into mid-late next week as a 1035-mb high over eastern \nCanada noses down into the Mid-Atlantic. This is evident after a\nbrief warm and humid pattern Sunday and Monday in the 80s to \naround 90. Ahead of the cooler and potentially more seasonal \npattern will be a cold front, sometime Monday or Tuesday, that \ncould bring showers/storms. Coupled to this will be a closed \nupper-low over the NE Gulf that could ride up the SE US in \nresponse to troughing over the MS/OH valley. If that were to \nhappen, suggested by some ensembles, as well as the EC-AIFS and \nAI-GFS, a surface low could develop along a frontal zone along \nthe Carolina coast, to favor isentropic rain or showers early to\nmiddle of next week. All of this is unclear given the spread in\nthe guidance, though there are signals for more seasonal \ntemperatures in the 70s to low 80s in a CAD-type regime if that \nwere to materialize.",
